2026-06-30 15:27:37,258 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-641c7e67-cc71-4b99-9b66-e8227117e0a3', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nRedox proteomics combined with proximity labeling enables monitoring of localized cysteine oxidation in cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nSummary\nReactive oxygen species (ROS) can modulate protein function through cysteine oxidation. Identifying protein targets of ROS can provide insight into uncharacterized ROS-regulated pathways. Several redox-proteomic workflows, such as OxICAT, exist to identify sites of cysteine oxidation. However, determining ROS targets localized within subcellular compartments and ROS hotspots remains challenging with existing workflows. Here, we present a chemoproteomic platform, PL-OxICAT, which combines proximity labeling (PL) with OxICAT to monitor localized cysteine oxidation events. We show that TurboID-based PL-OxICAT can monitor cysteine oxidation events within subcellular compartments such as the mitochondrial matrix and intermembrane space. Furthermore, we use APEX-based PL-OxICAT to monitor oxidation events within ROS hotspots by using endogenous ROS as the source of peroxide for APEX activation. Together, these platforms further hone our ability to monitor cysteine oxidation events within specific subcellular locations and ROS hotspots and provide a deeper understanding of the protein targets of endogenous and exogenous ROS.\nGraphical Abstract\neTOC Blurb\nMitochondrial cysteine oxidation regulates diverse pathways. Kisty et al., couple proximity labeling with redox proteomics to measure cysteine oxidation on mitochondrial proteins. Basal levels of oxidation on proteins localized in the matrix and intermembrane space are measured using TurboID. Cysteine oxidation within reactive oxygen species hotspots is determined using APEX.\n\n[METHODS]\nStar Methods\nResource Availability\nLead contact\nFurther information and requests for resources and reagents should be directed to and will be fulfilled by the lead contact, Eranthie Weerapana (eranthie@bc.edu).\nMaterials availability\nAll unique/stable reagents generated in this study are available from the lead contact with a completed Materials Transfer Agreement.\nData and Code Availability\nAll data reported in this paper will be shared by the lead contact upon request. The mass spectrometry proteomics data have been deposited to the ProteomeXchange Consortium via the PRIDE database (http://www.proteomexchange.org) and are publicly available as of the date of publication. Accession numbers are listed in the key resources table. Accession numbers are listed in the key resources table.\nThis paper does not report original code.\nAny additional information required to reanalyze the data reported in this paper is available from the lead contact upon request.\nExperimental model and subject details\nCell lines\nHEK 293T (CRL-3216, Homo sapiens, female, embryonic kidney) and Raw 264.7 cells (TIB-71, Mus musculus, male, adult macrophage) were obtained from the ATCC, cultured at 37°C under 5% CO2 in DMEM/high glucose with L-glutamine, sodium pyruvate (Cytiva) and supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (Bio-techne), 100 units/mL of penicillin, 100 μg/mL streptomycin, 0.25 μg/mL Amphotericin B (Gibco) and grown until a maximum passage of 25.\nMethod details\nCloning of TurboID and APEX2 plasmids\nTraditional cloning methods of PCR gene amplification, restriction enzyme digestion, ligation, and gateway cloning allowed for generation of pLenti APEX2 (matri-APEX and cyto-APEX) and TurboID (matrix-TID, IMS-TID, and cyto-TID) plasmids. For all plasmids, initial parent pDONR221 vectors containing MCS-TurboID-HA or MCS-V5-APEX2, respectively, were generated from PCR amplification using V5-TurboID-NES_pCDNA3 (addgene 107169) and mito-V5-APEX2 (addgene 72480), respectively, with incorporation of attb1/attb2 sites for gateway cloning into pDONR221 vectors. For pLenti matrix-TurboID and matrix-APEX plasmids, full length PDK1 (NM_002610) was PCR amplified with a N-terminal kozak sequence and inserted into pDONR221 MCS-TurboID-HA vector and pDONR221 MCS-V5-APEX2, respectively, using restriction enzyme digestion and ligation followed by gateway cloning into pLenti vectors. For pLenti IMS-TID, Gibson assembly was used to PCR amplify a codon optimized LACTB (1–68) leader sequence dsDNA fragment (Genewiz) with overhanging sequences for Gibson assembly into the pDONR221 MCS-TurboID-HA vector followed by gateway cloning into pLenti destination vector. All plasmids incorporated an N-terminal kozak sequence for mammalian translation. Mammalian protein expression was driven through CMV promoters in all pLenti plasmids.\nCell culture\nHEK 293T and Raw 264.7 cells were cultured at 37°C under 5% CO2 in DMEM/high glucose with L-glutamine, sodium pyruvate (Cytiva) and supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum (Bio-techne), 100 units/mL of penicillin, 100 μg/mL streptomycin, 0.25 μg/mL Amphotericin B (Gibco) and grown until a maximum passage of 25.\nGeneration of stable TID and APEX cell lines\nLentivirus was produced in HEK 293T cells, under passage 10, grown in DMEM and 10% heat inactivated fetal bovine serum (HIS) in 10 cm plates. At 80% confluency, cells were transfected with 2 μg vsv-g, 2 μg psPAX2, 2 μg respective pLenti plasmid and 24 μL of Lipofectamine 2000 (Invitrogen). After 24 hr, media was replaced by 5 mL DMEM with 10% HIS. At 48 hr, the media was collected and filtered through a 0.45 μm filter and mixed with 5 mL DMEM-HIS and 10 μg/mL polybrene. Virus was added to the target cell lines at 20% confluency and infected for 48 hr. Growth for 7 days in 2 μg/mL puromycin allowed for selection of stable protein overexpression in HEK 293T cells. The cyto-v5-APEX2 expressing Raw 264.7 cell line was grown for 7 days in 10 μg/mL puromycin and continuously passaged in 4 μg/mL puromycin to maintain APEX overexpressed populations.\nTurboID activation\nHEK 293T cells overexpressing matrix-TID, IMS-TID, and cyto-TID were plated on 10 cm dishes and grown to full confluency. Treatments with either increasing concentrations (0, 10, 100, 500, 1000 μM) of biotin in DMSO for 1 hr or 500 μM biotin in DMSO added to cell media for a variety of incubation times (0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1 hr) were quenched by washing 3 times with cold DPBS (Corning). For MS preparations, cells were treated with 500 μM biotin for 1 hr at 37°C. Reactions were quenched by washing 3 times with cold DPBS.\nMatrix-TID endogenous and exogenous ROS treatment\nHEK 293T overexpressing matrix-TID chimeras were treated with 500 μM biotin and 100 μM Antimycin A for 1 hr at 37°C for endogenous ROS evaluation or with 500 μM biotin for 1 hr at 37°C followed by removal of media and treatments with 1mM H2O2 in DPBS for 1 min for exogenous ROS evaluation. Biotinylation was quenched by washing 3 times with cold DPBS.\nAPEX activation by peroxide\nFor HEK 293T and Raw 264.7 cells overexpressing APEX chimeras, activation was evaluated through incubation with 500 μM biotin-phenol (Sigma) for 30 min at 37°C followed by removal of media and treatments with various increasing concentrations of H2O2 in DPBS for 1 min. Biotinylation was quenched by washing 3 times with quenching buffer (10 mM sodium azide, 10 mM sodium ascorbate and 5 mM Trolox) and 3 times with cold DPBS.\nAPEX activation by endogenous cellular ROS\nMatrix-APEX HEK 293T cells were treated with biotin-phenol for 45 min at 37°C. 100 μM Antimycin A/DMSO and DMSO control were added to the cells, respectively, and the treatment was conducted at 37°C for 45 min. APEX labeling was quenched by washes with quenching buffer as described above.\nCyto-APEX Raw 264.7 cells were incubated with 1 μg/mL LPS or no treatment, respectively, for 20 hr. Subsequently, 500 μM biotin-phenol was added for an additional 2.5 hr. Reactions were quenched by the same conditions described above.\nLysate Preparation\nCells were harvested by scraping and washed with DPBS (Corning). Cell pellets were resuspended in DPBS and lysed by ultrasonic tip sonication (Cole Parmer). Soluble lysate fractions were obtained after separation by centrifugation at 45,000 rpm for 45 min at 4°C pelleted insoluble fractions were discarded for cyto-TID, matrix-TID, and matrix-APEX HEK 293T cell lysates. For IMS-TID HEK 293T and cyto-APEX Raw 264.7 cell lysates, whole cell lysates were enriched by centrifugation at 4,500g for 10 min at 4°C with supernatant collected. To determine protein concentrations, DC Protein Assay kit (Bio-Rad) was performed.\nImmunoblotting\nAll samples were separated by 12.5% SDS-PAGE gels and transferred to nitrocellulose membranes by electrophoresis at 75 volts for 140 min. Membranes were washed with tris-buffered saline with 1% Tween:20 (TBS-T) 3 times for 5 min followed by staining with Ponceau S (2 min in 0.1% w/v Ponceau S in 5% acetic acid/water) and imaged on a ChemiDoc MP imaging system (BioRad, Hercules, CA). After 3 washes with TBS-T, membranes were blocked with 5% (w/v) non-fat dry milk in TBS-T at room temperature for 1 hr. Following 3 additional washes with TBS-T, membranes were incubated with respective primary antibodies overnight at 4°C. Following removal and an additional 3 washes with TBS-T, the membranes were coated with secondary HRP-conjugated antibodies for 1 hr at room temperature. For biotin blots, membranes were blocked in 5% milk overnight at 4°C and after washes, were incubated with 200 ng/mL streptavidin-HRP in TBS-T for 1 hr. After final washes with TBS-T, membranes were developed with HRP-super signal chemiluminescence reagents (Thermo Fisher) and visualized as described above.\nImmunohistochemistry and immunofluorescence\nImmunofluorescence of Stable Cell Lines\nFor fluorescence microscopy imaging, cells were grown on 35 mm glass bottom dishes (MatTek) that were treated with 1 mg/mL poly-D-lysine (Sigma) incubated for 5 min at 37°C and washed 3 times with DPBS. Without exposure to light, cells were treated with 50 nM MitoTracker Red CMXRos (Invitrogen) in RPMI medium without phenol red (Gibco) for 45 min at 37°C. Cells were washed 3 times with DPBS for 5 min and fixed with 4% formaldehyde (Thermo Fisher Pierce) in DPBS for 15 min at room temperature. After 3 additional washes with DPBS for 5 min, cells were blocked with blocking buffer of 5% normal goat serum (Cell Signaling Technology) and 0.3% Triton X-100 in DPBS for 1 hr at room temperature. After washing 3 times with DPBS, cells were incubated with primary antibodies diluted in 1% BSA and 0.3% Triton X-100 in DPBS overnight at 4°C. Cells were washed 3 times with DPBS and incubated with secondary antibodies conjugated to AlexaFluor488 for 2 hr at room temperature. After the final 3 washes with DPBS for 5 min, Prolong Gold Antifade Reagent with DAPI (Cell Signaling Technology) was applied and the samples were cured overnight at room temperature. A coverslip was added and sealed with clear nail polish before imaging with a Leica TCS SP5 scanning confocal microscope.\nAntimycin A Superoxide Immunofluorescence Assay\nHeLa cells were grown on 35 mm glass bottom dishes (MatTek) that were treated with 1 mg/mL poly-D-lysine (Sigma) incubated for 5 min at 37°C and washed 3 times with DPBS. Cells were incubated with 0, 6, 12.5, 25, and 50 μM Antimycin A/DMSO in DMEM for 45 min at 37°C. The cells were then washed with HBSS without phenol red (Gibco) and incubated with 1 μM MitoSOX (Invitrogen) and 200 μM DAPI (Invitrogen) for 10 min at 37°C without exposure to light. After washing 3 times with HBSS, the plates were imaged on a Zeiss Axio Observer fluorescence microscope.\nPL-OxICAT MS Preparation\nOxICAT sample preparations were adapted from Bechtel, et al. and Topf, et al. 4 mg total protein for matrix-TID and IMS-TID and 2 mg total protein for cyto-TID cell lysates were precipitated by TCA at −80°C for 1 hr or overnight. Samples were pelleted at 15,000 rpm for 10 min and resuspended in 500 μL acetone. After centrifugation at 5,000 rpm for 10 min, sample pellets were resuspended and solubilized in 250 μL denaturing-alkylation buffer (DAB) (6 M Urea, 200 mM Tris-HCl, 10 mM EDTA and 0.5% SDS, pH 8.5) containing 10 mM light-NEM (5 μL of 500 mM Light-NEM, Sigma). Reduced cysteines were alkylated in the dark at 37°C for 2 hr with shaking. Samples were centrifuged at 21,000 g for 1 min to pellet any precipitant and supernatant was collected and diluted 3-fold with water (500 μL) to lower the urea content. Proteins were precipitated with 5-fold volume of ice-cold acetone (3.75 mL) and incubated at −20°C for 2 hr or overnight. After precipitation, samples were pelleted at 4500 g for 30 min, 4°C. Pellets were washed with 500 μL acetone and centrifuged at 4500 g for 10 min. Samples were solubilized in 160 μL DAB and cysteines were reduced with 8 μL of 50 mM tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP, 2.5 mM final concentration). Samples were agitated at 37°C for 5 min. After reduction, 240 μL DAB was added to dilute TCEP and newly reduced cysteines were labeled by the addition of 10 mM heavy-NEM (8 μL of 500 mM D5-NEM, Cambridge Isotope Laboratories) incubate 2 hr at 37°C in the dark. After the final alkylation, samples were diluted 3-fold with water (800 μL) and 5-fold total volume of acetone (6 mL) was added and proteins precipitated at −20°C for 2 hr or overnight. Proteins were pelleted by centrifugation at 4500 g for 30 min at 4°C and washed with 500 μL acetone. Following centrifugation at 4500 g for 10 min, labeled-protein pellets were resuspended in 1 mL 1.2% (w/v) SDS/PBS using sonication. Solubilized samples were diluted to 0.2% SDS with the addition of 5mL PBS containing 100 μL of washed streptavidin agarose resin (Thermo Scientific) and rotated overnight at 4°C. SDS solutions were resolubilized at room temperature for 2.5 hr and the resin was incubated with 0.2% SDS/PBS for 10 min followed by 3 washes with PBS and 3 washes with water (5 mL each). Resin was pelleted by centrifugation (1400 g for 3 min) and supernatant removed between each wash step. After the final wash step, resin was resuspended in 200 μL of 2 M Urea and 1 mM CaCl2. 2 μg of trypsin (Promega) was added, and the samples digested overnight at 37°C with agitation. The supernatant was collected, and resin was washed 3 times with 50 μL water and combined generating a total sample volume of 350 μL. Trypsin digestion was quenched by the addition of 17.5 μL formic acid (5% final concentration). For initial matrix-TID samples and all cyto-TID samples, cysteines were isotopically labeled by light-IA and heavy-IA (made in house) instead of light-NEM and heavy-NEM.\nMS Proteomics\nPeptide analysis was achieved by Multidimensional Protein Identification Technology (MudPIT) on an LTQ Orbitrap Discovery mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher) with sample separation coupled to an Agilent 1200 series HPLC (Agilent Technologies), or on an Orbitrap Exploris 240.\nFor analysis on the Orbitrap Discovery, peptides were loaded onto a self-packed reverse phase C18 resin 250 μm fused-silica capillary column. 5 Gradients of 100% Buffer B in Buffer A (Buffer A: 95% water, 5% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid; Buffer B: 20% water, 80% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid) with 5 salt pushes, eluted peptides onto a biphasic 100 μm fused-silica capillary column with a 5 μm tip packed with 10 cm of C18 resin and 4 cm of Partisphere strong cation exchange resin (SCX, Whatman). Instrumentation operated at a flow rate of ~0.25 μL/min buffer through the column and spray voltage of 2.75 kV. One full MS scan (400 – 1800 MW) was followed by 7 data dependent scans of the nth most intense ions with dynamic exclusion enabled.\nFor analysis on the Orbitrap Exploris, trypsin digested peptide samples were dried down by speed vac and resuspended in 30 μL 100% H2O, 0.1 % formic acid. Peptide analysis was then achieved using an Orbitrap Exploris 240 mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific) coupled to a Dionex Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano system (Thermo Scientific). 5μL of peptide sample was injected directly onto an Acclaim PepMap 100 loading column. Peptides were eluted onto an Acclaim PepMap RSLC and separated with a 160 min gradient of 5%–99% Buffer B in Buffer A (Buffer A: 100% H2O, 0.1% formic acid; Buffer B: 80% acetonitrile, 20% H2O, 0.1% formic acid). Instrumentation operated at a flow rate of 0.3 μL/min buffer through the column and spray voltage of 2.1 kV. One full MS scan (350–1800 m/z, 120,000 resolution, RF lens 65%, AGC target 300%, automatic maximum injection time, profile mode) was obtained every 2 seconds with dynamic exclusion (repeat count 2, duration 10 s), isotopic exclusion (assigned), and apex detection (30% desired apex window) enabled. A variable number of MS2 scans (15,000 resolution, AGC 75%, maximum injection time 100 ms, centroid mode) were obtained between each MS1 scan based on the highest precursor masses, filtered for monoisotopic peak determination, theoretical precursor isotopic envelope fit, intensity (5E4), and charge state (2–6). MS2 analysis consisted of the isolation of precursor ions (isolation window 2 m/z) followed by higher-energy collision dissociation (HCD) (collision energy 30%).\nMS Proteomic Analysis\nMass spectrometry data acquired on the Orbitrap Discovery were searched using a SEQUEST algorithm against a concatenated target/decoy non-redundant variant of Homo sapiens or Mus musculus FASTA databases containing UniProt descriptions. Dynamic modifications on cysteine were included to survey the alkylation by either light-NEM (+125.04766) and heavy-NEM (+130.07906) or light-IA (+185.08406) and heavy-IA (+191.10419). Proteins were identified in matched MS spectra, packaged, and filtered using DTASelect2.0. Spectra were sorted with options for -trypstat applied for analyzing tryptic protein enrichment and -modstat applied for specific analysis of labeled cysteine-containing peptides and were restricted to a 5% maximum false discovery rate. For analysis of proximity-labeling enrichment, protein spectral counts were pooled by an in-house Python program with additional annotations for localization and function.\nOxICAT specific analysis\nFurthermore, all identified cysteine-containing peptides were delimited to having full tryptic cleavage (-y 2), required at least one peptide per locus (-p 1) and a delta-CN score greater than 0.06 (-d 0.06). Cysteine residue position, function, species conservation, domain, and subcellular localizations were annotated by an in-house Python program. Characterized mitochondrial localization was determined by UniProt and MitoCarta2.046 specifications. Heavy:light (H:L) and light:heavy (L:H) ratios were resolved using cimage quantification package. L:H and H:L ratios were converted to percent oxidation values by the following equations:  and . All considered peptides were presented in at least 2 replicates. Average percent oxidation values were calculated for each peptide and standard deviations >30% were removed from the final compilation of labeled-peptides analyses.\nAnalysis of MS data collected on the Orbitrap Exploris 240 was performed using Thermo Proteome Discoverer software (PD; version 2.4.1.15). Protein identification was achieved using the SequestHT and Percolator algorithms against a Homo sapiens proteome UniProtKB database (SwissProt TaxID=9606). The protease enzyme was set as trypsin with a maximum of 2 missed cleavages allowed. The peptide precursor mass tolerance was set to 10 ppm with a fragment mass tolerance of 0.02 Da. Dynamic modifications included methionine oxidation (+ 15.995), N-terminal acetylation (+42.011) and/or methionine loss (−131.040), and cysteine alkylation by either light-NEM (+125.048) and heavy-NEM (+130.079). The false discovery rate (FDR) for highly confident peptide identification was set at 1%. The resulting L:H and H:L ratios were converted to percent oxidation values by the following equations:  and . All considered peptides were presented in at least 2 replicates, other than peptides with singleton ratio values (R = 100 or 0.1), which were required to have the same value in all three replicates. Average percent oxidation values were calculated for each peptide and standard deviations >30% were removed from the final compilation of labeled-peptides analyses.\nStatistical analysis of overrepresented gene ontology (GO) process using PANTHER, GOrilla, and ReviGO.\nPanther overrepresentation gene ontology tests (http://pantherdb.org) were performed on all TurboID and APEX-enriched protein datasets. Enriched processes were determined by GOrilla using a ranked gene ontology (GO) analysis and FDR corrected for multiple testing using Benjamini and Hochberg and Fisher methods. Enrichment (E) was defined in terms of the number of intersecting genes (b) over the number of top genes in the target set (n) divided by the total number of genes correlated to a unique GO term (B) over the total number of genes analyzed (N) (E = (b/n) / (B/N). Enriched terms were sorted by the number of top genes (n) and redundant terms were manually removed after analysis by ReviGO for all datasets except for matrix-APEX and cyto-APEX.\nQuantification and statistical analysis\nAll final MS data sets are expressed as the average value of triplicate or quadruplicate (data pertaining to Raw 264.7 expressing cyto-APEX) biological replicates with standard deviation < 20% for any value with fold change > 2. GraphPad Prism 9.4.1 was used to analyze the significance between sample groups. Statistical analyses of samples versus negative controls were performed using unpaired two-tailed t-tests. In all cases, significance is indicated as follows: *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01, ***p < 0.001, ns for not significant. Error bars represent standard deviation. Details of individual statistical analyses are provided within each figure legend.\nData S1. Excel file containing all Gene Ontology analyses, lists of proteins identified in each mass spectrometry analysis, and percent oxidation values for all cysteines. Related to Figures 1–5.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\nTargeting TurboID to mitochondrial sub-compartments\nTo implement the PL-OxICAT platform to monitor cysteine oxidation in the mitochondrial matrix and IMS, we localized TurboID to the mitochondrial matrix and IMS (Figure 1A). Specifically, matrix-localized TurboID (matrix-TID) was generated by fusing TurboID to pyruvate dehydrogenase kinase 1 (PDK1), a matrix-residing kinase. A PDK1-APEX2 fusion was previously shown to selectively biotinylate matrix-localized proteins. IMS-localized TurboID (IMS-TID) was obtained by fusing TurboID to a lactamase beta (LACTB) leader sequence that was previously used for APEX studies of the IMS proteome. Additionally, a cytosol-localized TurboID construct (cyto-TID) was generated as a cytosolic biotinylation control. All of the constructs contained the desired localization sequence at the N-terminus and an HA tag on the C-terminus of TurboID. HEK 293T cells with stable expression of the TurboID proteins were generated using lentiviral transduction. Proper cellular localization of each TurboID protein was confirmed by confocal fluorescence microscopy (Figure 2A). Both matrix-TID and IMS-TID protein expression profiles co-localized with the MitoTracker stain. In contrast, cyto-TID displayed no mitochondrial localization, instead displaying the expected widespread distribution of TurboID in the cytosol.\nUpon confirming the desired cellular localization, the biotinylation efficiencies of matrix-TID and cyto-TID were determined by treating cells with varying biotin concentrations (Figure S1) for different incubation times (Figure 2B). Maximal biotinylation for matrix-TID was observed when treating cells with 500 μM biotin for 1 hour. As expected, the levels of biotinylation for cyto-TID were significantly higher than that observed for matrix-TID, likely due to the higher concentration and larger diversity of proteins in the cytosol relative to the mitochondria. Detectable levels of protein biotinylation were also observed for IMS-TID using these optimized biotinylation conditions (Figure 2C).\nPL-OxICAT with TurboID enables quantification of cysteine oxidation events within the mitochondrial matrix and IMS\nUpon confirming mitochondrial localization and successful protein biotinylation by the three TurboID constructs, we then devised a workflow to combine PL, which enriches proteins proximal to the expressed TurboID construct, with OxICAT, to quantify the level of cysteine oxidation on these enriched proteins (Figure 1B). Cells expressing cyto-TID, matrix-TID and IMS-TID were incubated with 500 μM biotin for 1 hour to biotinylate proteins within the desired subcellular regions. After cell lysis, and protein denaturation, all reduced cysteine thiols were capped with isotopically light NEM or iodoacetamide-alkyne (IA-light). Reversibly oxidized cysteine thiols were then reduced using tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP) and subsequently reacted with isotopically heavy NEM or IA-heavy. The biotinylated proteins generated by each of the TurboID constructs were then enriched on streptavidin resin and subjected to an on-bead trypsin digestion. The resulting tryptic peptides were analyzed by LC/LC-MS/MS to generate corresponding peptide and protein identifications. Protein identifications were parsed to provide information on the nature of the proteins localized proximal to each TurboID construct. The individual peptide identifications were then filtered for cysteine-containing peptides, for which the abundance of the isotopically light versus heavy species was quantified in the form of a light:heavy ratio. These light:heavy ratio values were used to determine the percentage of oxidation for each identified cysteine residue within the sample.\nWe first focused our attention on the protein identifications obtained from the MS analyses to assess the subcellular localization of proteins biotinylated by each of the three TurboID constructs. Protein identifications from the matrix-TID, IMS-TID, and cyto-TID samples (Data S1) were compared to mitochondrial annotations reported by UniProt, MitoCarta2.0, and previous matrix and IMS proteins identified using APEX PL. The matrix-TID sample generated 1180 protein identifications of which 450 (38%) are annotated as mitochondria localized. Given that the matrix-TID construct was a fusion to PDK1, we identified known PDK1 interactors, including DLD, DLAT, and PDK2/3/4, confirming the proximity-mediated biotinylation of PDK1 proximal proteins in the matrix. Similarly, 1980 proteins were identified in the IMS-TID analysis, of which 456 (23%) are mitochondria localized. In contrast, the cyto-TID sample generated 1765 total proteins, but only 126 (7%) are annotated as localized to the mitochondria (Figure 3A). The difference was even more pronounced when comparing peptide spectral counts across the three samples, where 75% and 36% of the matrix-TID and IMS-TID spectral counts originated from mitochondrial proteins, compared to 6% in the cyto-TID sample (Figure 3A). A higher fraction of the proteins identified in the IMS-TID sample compared to the matrix-TID sample (77% versus 62%) were localized outside of the mitochondria. This is likely due to diffusion of the biotin-AMP intermediate through the outer mitochondrial membrane, which is known to be significantly more permeable than the inner mitochondrial membrane. Direct comparison of matrix-TID and IMS-TID samples to the cyto-TID sample clearly demonstrates that mitochondrial proteins were identified with higher average spectral counts in the matrix-TID and IMS-TID samples (Figure 3B). To further confirm enrichment of the desired mitochondrial compartments, we performed gene ontology (GO) analysis of the proteins identified in the matrix-TID, IMS-TID and cyto-TID datasets. Our matrix-TID data revealed enrichment of known mitochondrial processes including carboxylic acid catabolism and mitochondrial RNA metabolism (Data S1). Similarly, the IMS-TID data showed enrichment of mitochondrial protein transport, ATP transmembrane transport, and protein refolding (Data S1). In contrast, GO analysis of the cyto-TID dataset resulted in no enrichment of known mitochondrial processes (Data S1). Together, these data serve to confirm that the use of matrix-TID and IMS-TID constructs successfully enrich proteins annotated as mitochondria localized. Lastly, matrix-TID and IMS-TID samples were compared to their respective control samples where biotin was omitted. As expected, both control samples without biotin contained significantly fewer protein identifications (Data S1, Figure S2A and S2B).\nWe compared the protein identifications we obtained using matrix-TID and IMS-TID to previous proteome datasets obtained using either mitochondrial fractionation or APEX PL. When compared to mitochondrial fractionation, our matrix-TID and IMS-TID analyses generated more total mitochondrial protein identifications (Figure S2C and S2D). The increased coverage was particularly noticeable for annotated IMS proteins, which are often low abundant and challenging to identify in whole-mitochondria analyses (Figure S2E). This improved coverage is noteworthy because relative to the mitochondrial fractionation methods, the TurboID enrichment is significantly less labor intensive, and requires reduced cell input. Our protein coverage from matrix-TID and IMS-TID were also comparable to previous matrix and IMS-targeted APEX PL studies. Our matrix-TID data identified 375 (64%) of the mitochondrial proteins identified using matrix-targeted APEX (Figure S2F). Similarly, an IMS-targeted APEX study identified 136 putative IMS proteins, and 63 (46%) of these were identified in our IMS-TID analysis (Figure S2G). Together, these comparisons to previous mitochondria-targeting PL studies show that our constructs are similarly enriching the desired subset of mitochondrial proteins.\nUpon confirming successful enrichment and identification of proteins within the matrix and IMS, we then focused on the individual cysteine-containing peptides that were identified. The matrix-TID data identified 1710 cysteine peptides, with 885 residing on annotated mitochondrial proteins (Data S1). Since the samples were processed using the OxICAT workflow, reduced cysteine thiols were labeled with an isotopically ‘light’ cysteine-capping reagent, and reversibly oxidized cysteines were labeled with the corresponding ‘heavy’ reagent. Therefore, light:heavy ratios can be used to determine the percent oxidation of each cysteine residue. We filtered the identified mitochondrial cysteine peptides for those that afforded light:heavy ratios in two of three replicates, resulting in 277 cysteines for which corresponding percent oxidation values were obtained with high confidence (Figure 3C, Data S1) in the matrix-TID dataset. Given the highly reducing environment of the mitochondrial matrix, the majority (96%) of these cysteines were found in a highly reduced state (less than 20% oxidation). There were 10 cysteines with greater than 20% oxidation, and of these, only one cysteine, C48 from 5’-nucleotidase domain-containing protein 2 (NT5DC2) was found to be highly oxidized at 95%. NT5DC2 is a poorly characterized putative mitochondrial matrix localized nucleotidase. Analysis of the available AlphaFold structure for NT5DC2, indicate proximity of C48 to two other cysteine residues, C502 and C507, that could potentially engage in disulfide bond formation with this cysteine.\nThe IMS-TID sample generated 2151 cysteine-containing peptides, of which 613 were derived from mitochondrial proteins (Data S1). High confidence percent oxidation values were determined for a total of 196 cysteines (Figure 3D, Data S1). Similar to the matrix-TID data, the majority (96%) were highly reduced with less than 20% oxidation. There were 9 cysteines with greater than 20% oxidation, and of these 6 cysteines were found to be greater than 50% oxidized. The cysteines that were highly oxidized included several that are annotated to be engaged in disulfide-bond formation. For example, C65 and C30 in the cytochrome c oxidase subunit 6B1 (COX6B1), a subunit of ETC complex IV, were found to be 79% and 82% oxidized, respectively. These cysteines are located within signature CX9C and CX10C motifs, which have been shown to form intramolecular disulfide bonds on the IMS face of this mitochondrial inner membrane protein. Additionally, C36 in the NADH dehydrogenase 1 alpha subcomplex subunit 8, NDUFA8, was found to be 56% oxidized. This cysteine is annotated to form an intramolecular disulfide bond with C46, within a CX9C motif.\nThe IMS is known to be a significantly more oxidizing environment than the matrix, thereby supporting the sustained presence of proteins with disulfide bonds. We compared the extent of cysteine oxidation on annotated matrix and IMS proteins to determine if the differential redox potentials are reflected in the global oxidation states of cysteines localized within these compartments. This comparison revealed that the average percent oxidation of IMS cysteine residues is ~23.64%, and the average percent oxidation for matrix cysteines is ~6.157% (Figure 3E), thereby confirming the increased oxidative environment of the IMS.\nLastly, from the corresponding cyto-TID data, percent oxidation was calculated for 580 cysteines, of which 30 are annotated as mitochondrial and 275 as cytoplasmic (Figure 3F, Data S1). Of the cytoplasmic cysteines identified, the majority (95%) were determined to be less than 20% reduced, reflecting the highly reducing environment of the cytosol. Together, these data demonstrate the ability of PL-OxICAT to quantify the percent oxidation of matrix and IMS-localized cysteines under basal cellular conditions, and specifically identify structural disulfides localized within the IMS.\nIn addition to monitoring basal levels of cysteine oxidation in the mitochondrial matrix, we evaluated how these cysteine oxidation states change upon induction of oxidative stress. Cells were treated with two sources of oxidants; the first was a bolus dose of exogenous hydrogen peroxide, and the second was the induction of endogenous ROS using the ETC inhibitor antimycin A (AMA), a complex III Q-cycle inhibitor, known to increase ROS levels in the mitochondrial matrix. We then compared the levels of cysteine oxidation observed under each of these conditions (Figure 3G, Data S1). As expected, treatment of cells with exogenous hydrogen peroxide resulted in a large shift in the average percent oxidation of identified cysteines. In contrast, AMA treatment only resulted in a minor increase in the average percent oxidation, due to the lower concentrations and more localized ROS generation expected under these conditions. Together, we demonstrate that PL-OxICAT can be used to monitor cysteine oxidation levels under both basal and oxidizing cellular conditions.\nPL-OxICAT with APEX enables quantification of cysteine oxidation events within ROS hotspots\nUpon demonstrating the utility of PL-OxICAT with TurboID to enrich and quantify cysteine oxidation within specific subcellular compartments, we sought to develop a complementary technology that would allow for selective monitoring of protein oxidation within ROS hotspots. ROS hotspots are regions of high levels of ROS that typically surround the source of ROS generation. The diffusion distance of hydrogen peroxide in the cell depends on the presence of membranes that could hamper free diffusion, and the presence of proteins that could consume that peroxide (e.g. peroxiredoxins). Here we shifted our focus to the PL method APEX, which uses a peroxidase that is typically activated by exogenous hydrogen peroxide. We hypothesized that the exogenous hydrogen peroxide could be replaced with endogenously produced hydrogen peroxide to activate APEX and biotinylate proteins proximal to the source of ROS. Since ROS levels are elevated within ROS hotspots, we sought to determine if these endogenous ROS levels were sufficiently high to activate APEX selectively within these localized ROS hotspots. We first focused on ROS hotspots generated upon AMA treatment. Since activation of APEX by endogenous ROS has not previously been demonstrated, we initially focused on localizing APEX to the mitochondrial matrix to determine if AMA treatment would sufficiently increase hydrogen peroxide levels to induce APEX activation and subsequent biotinylation of mitochondrial proteins.\nTo localize APEX to the mitochondrial matrix, we generated a construct whereby APEX2 was fused to the C-terminus of PDK1 to produce a PDK1-V5-APEX2 protein, termed matrix-APEX. A cytosol localized construct (cyto-APEX) was also generated as a control that would express APEX only within the cytosol. HEK 293T cells stably expressing either matrix-APEX and cyto-APEX were generated using lentiviral transduction. Immunofluorescence imaging using an anti-V5 antibody confirmed expression and mitochondrial localization of matrix-APEX (Figure 4A). In contrast, cyto-APEX was shown to be expressed only within the cytosol (Figure 4A). We then confirmed that both APEX constructs were functional by evaluating biotinylation upon treatment with biotin-phenol and exogenous hydrogen peroxide. Matrix-APEX and cyto-APEX expressing cells pre-treated with biotin-phenol were treated for 1 min with increasing concentrations of hydrogen peroxide, prior to cell lysis and evaluation of biotinylation using western blot analysis (Figure S3). The level of biotinylation increased with peroxide concentration for both constructs, confirming that the APEX constructs were functionally active. Notably, the banding patterns of biotinylated proteins were significantly different between cyto-APEX and matrix-APEX, serving as further confirmation that these APEX constructs were expressed in different subcellular locations. Importantly, standard APEX experiments are performed using 1 mM hydrogen peroxide treatment, however, we observed detectable levels of biotinylation at concentrations as low as 100 μM. The observation that APEX-mediated biotinylation can occur at lower and more physiologically relevant hydrogen peroxide concentrations, known to be in the picomolar to micromolar range, supported the potential of using endogenous ROS sources for APEX activation.\nUpon confirming activation of matrix-APEX with exogenous hydrogen peroxide, we next evaluated the effectiveness of endogenous ROS as an APEX activator. Matrix-APEX expressing cells were treated with biotin-phenol prior to inducing ROS generation. To induce ROS generation in the mitochondria, we treated cells with the ETC inhibitor, AMA, and monitored protein biotinylation using western blot analysis. The concentrations of AMA used (100 μM AMA) was selected to generate maximal ROS without the induction of cell death (Figure S4). AMA treatment led to a significant increase in protein biotinylation (Figure 4B). These studies confirm that the endogenous ROS generated by AMA-mediated inhibition of complex III is sufficient to generate detectable levels of protein biotinylation through activation of matrix-localized APEX.\nWe then proceeded to use the PL-OxICAT platform to identify biotinylated proteins and quantify the percentage of cysteine oxidation within ROS hotspots resulting from AMA treatment. Briefly, cells pre-incubated with biotin-phenol were then treated with AMA and subjected to the OxICAT workflow described previously (Figure 1B). The resulting MS analysis identified 1322 proteins, of which 389 (29%) were localized to the mitochondria (Figure 4C, Data S1). MS analysis of the corresponding control sample that was not treated with AMA generated significantly fewer protein identifications (705 total proteins, 175 mitochondrial proteins), since APEX is likely minimally activated without a significant influx of hydrogen peroxide (Figure 4C, Data S1). GO analysis confirmed enrichment of mitochondria-localized pathways, including oxidation-reduction processes (Data S1). Comparing the total proteins identified in the matrix-APEX data to our matrix-TID data showed 961 proteins shared across these two PL methods, with 310 overlapping mitochondrial proteins (Figure S5A). These data confirm that matrix-APEX is localized to the mitochondria and activation by endogenous ROS produced by AMA treatment results in selective biotinylation of mitochondrial proteins.\nWe then focused on cysteine-containing peptides identified within the AMA-treated matrix-APEX dataset. A total of 2200 cysteines were identified, of which 883 were cysteines that resided on 271 mitochondrial proteins (Data S1). Filtering these hits for those cysteines for which light:heavy ratios were determined across at least two replicates, generated percent oxidation values for 427 cysteines (Figure 4D, Data S1). The majority of identified cysteines (75%) were found to be highly reduced (<20% oxidation), with 4% of cysteines (16) found to be in a highly oxidized (>50% oxidized) state. Within this subset of highly oxidized cysteines were several well-characterized sites of cysteine oxidation. For example, oxidation of C385 in aconitase 2 (ACO2) is known to inhibit enzymatic activity through loss of a critical [4Fe-4S] cofactor. Additionally, C47, the peroxidatic cysteine of peroxiredoxin 6 (PRDX6), is known to become oxidized to sulfenic acid in the presence of ROS. The average percent oxidation of cysteine residues was compared between the AMA treated and untreated samples. As expected, AMA treatment results in a significant increase in ROS levels that is reflected in the higher average percent oxidation of cysteines (Figure 4E, Data S1).\nThe cysteine oxidation events determined in the matrix-APEX sample with AMA treatment was compared to the corresponding matrix-TID sample with AMA treatment. One important difference between these two analyses is that the cysteines enriched in the APEX sample are located proximal to ROS hotspots in the mitochondria, whereas cysteines in the TID sample are distributed across the entire mitochondrial matrix. This difference is reflected in the average percent oxidation for identified cysteines, whereby cysteines identified in the matrix-APEX analysis displayed higher levels of oxidation than cysteines from the matrix-TID analysis (Figure 4F).\nWe then compared the biotinylation profile and percent oxidation for mitochondrial cysteines upon induction of endogenous versus exogenous ROS. Matrix-APEX cells were treated with 1 mM hydrogen peroxide for 1 min to generate global cellular ROS. We then compared the cysteine oxidation profiles upon exogenous hydrogen peroxide treatment to endogenous ROS generation via AMA treatment. There was 86% and 56% overlap in the mitochondrial proteins and cysteines identified across these two datasets, respectively (Figure S5B). Within the subset of 206 cysteines with calculated percent oxidation values that overlapped, we observed an overall increase in oxidation in the AMA-treated sample, relative to the hydrogen peroxide-treated sample (Figure 4G, Data S1). The AMA-treated matrix-APEX analysis focused on cysteines localized within ROS hotspots where ROS levels are elevated, whereas the exogenous hydrogen peroxide-treated matrix-APEX analysis looks at all cysteines within the entire mitochondrial matrix. It is therefore to be expected that higher levels of oxidation would be observed within ROS hotspots with elevated ROS levels, relative to the entire mitochondria, where the ROS are more widely distributed. Together, our data identify proteins localized within ROS hotspots in the mitochondrial matrix and show that cysteine oxidation events are magnified within these ROS hotspots.\nPL-OxICAT with APEX identifies cysteine oxidation events induced by macrophage differentiation\nTo demonstrate the generality of PL-OxICAT with APEX to other ROS hotspots outside of the mitochondria, we induced endogenous ROS through activation of NADPH oxidase (NOX) in Raw 264.7 mouse macrophages. It is known that cytokine treatment results in NOX activation and generation of cellular hydrogen peroxide that subsequently regulates a transcriptional program essential for macrophage differentiation. The ROS generated by NOX is released extracellularly but is known to diffuse into the cytosol to generate intracellular ROS hotspots proximal to the plasma membrane. We therefore generated a Raw 264.7 cell line that stably expressed cyto-APEX. Cytosolic localization was confirmed by immunofluorescence (Figure 5A). Macrophage activation was initiated by treatment with lipopolysaccharides (LPS). Previous studies reported that peroxide concentrations reach a maximum after 20 hours of LPS treatment. Based on these reports, the cyto-APEX Raw 264.7 cell line was treated with LPS for 20 hours, prior to addition of biotin-phenol. Protein biotinylation was monitored by western blot, and an observable biotinylation signal was detected upon LPS treatment, confirming that NOX-generated ROS levels are sufficiently high to activate APEX (Figure 5B).\nWe then proceeded to perform the PL-OxICAT analysis, where cyto-APEX-expressing Raw 264.7 cells were treated with and without LPS for 20 hours, followed by biotin-phenol addition. Cell lysates were subjected to the OxICAT workflow as described previously. MS analysis identified 1627 proteins in the LPS-treated sample, compared to 376 proteins in the absence of LPS (Figure 5C, Data S1). This increase in biotinylated proteins upon LPS treatment is expected since the increased ROS levels lead to APEX activation and enhanced biotinylation relative to the untreated sample. The majority of identified proteins (66%) were annotated as cytosolic proteins, confirming the cytosolic localization of cyto-APEX. Interestingly, 423 plasma membrane proteins were identified to be biotinylated with the cyto-APEX construct, which is expected since NOX is membrane localized, and the highest levels of ROS are likely to be localized proximal to the cell membrane. Furthermore, GO analysis of the proteins identified in the cyto-APEX Raw 264.7 cells revealed enrichment for cytosolic and differentiation-related processes including regulation of type-I interferon production, cytoplasmic translation initiation, and glycolysis (Data S1).\nThe PL-OxICAT analysis of cyto-APEX in the presence of LPS identified 1777 cysteine-containing peptides (Data S1). Of these, light:heavy ratios and corresponding percent oxidation values were calculated for 599 cysteines (Figure 5D, Data S1). Of these cysteines, 499 were >50% oxidized. Examples of highly oxidized cysteines include C480 and C482 of the NOX-promoting inflammatory messenger (NOTCH2), which were 95% oxidized. These cysteines are annotated as extracellular disulfides. Additionally, four cysteines, C33, C857, C938 and C629, on inducible nitric oxide synthase (NOS2) were identified to be 68–87% oxidized. NOS2 expression is known to be induced by NOX activation and LPS stimulation. Furthermore, we identify high levels of oxidation (94%) of an extracellular disulfide-bonded cysteine, C353 on toll-like receptor 2 (TRL2) which is known to mediate innate immunity and inflammatory responses to bacterial lipopeptides by activating MYD88 resulting in cytokine release through nuclear factor-κB (NF-κB) and mitogen activating protein kinase (MAPK). Identification of these previously characterized oxidized cysteines on proteins known to localize with NOX, where ROS levels are highest, serve to confirm the ability of our PL-OxICAT strategy to enrich proteins within ROS hotspots.\nOutside of annotated structural disulfides on extracellular domains of plasma-membrane proteins, we identified several intracellular cysteines displaying high levels of oxidation. For example, multiple cysteines were identified to be oxidized on the detoxifying enzyme peroxiredoxin 4 (PRDX4), including the resolving cysteine, C248 (65% oxidized). Additionally, non-catalytic cysteines C54 and C151 on PRDX4 were also identified to be 83% and 52% oxidized, respectively. These cysteines are implicated in oligomerization and activation of PRDX4 under oxidizing conditions. PRDX4 is known to mediate redox signaling events in the cytosol upon LPS stimulation of macrophages, and our data demonstrates the presence and increased oxidation of PRDX4 within ROS hotspots generated during pro-inflammatory signaling. Additionally, we identified highly oxidized cysteines on several proteins involved in glycolysis (Figure 5E). For example, we identified C245 on glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) to be 74% oxidized. This cysteine is known to be a site of S-nitrosation and sulfinylation in human GAPDH. The presence of glycolytic enzymes proximal to NOX is notable it has been shown that glycolytic enzymes can form complexes that localize to the cytosolic face of the plasma membrane, where these enzymes can provide localized ATP pools required for increased NOX activity. Many of these glycolytic enzymes have previously been identified as targets of oxidation. Lastly, several phosphatases and kinases involved in regulating the cell cycle and cellular differentiation were identified as oxidized. For example, C285 on receptor-type tyrosine-protein phosphatase C (PTPRC), a central receptor in T-cell activation upon cytokine engagement, was identified to be 38% oxidized. Additionally, C196 and C251 on serine/threonine-protein phosphatase 2A (PPP2CB), a modulator of a variety of kinases including ERK1/2 MAPK signaling cascades, was found to be 70% and 54% oxidized, respectively. Together, our data demonstrate that cysteines on a diverse array of proteins involved in redox regulation, metabolism, and phosphorylation, are localized and oxidized within ROS hotspots during immune-cell activation. Lastly, the average percent oxidation of cysteine residues was compared between the LPS treated and untreated samples. As expected, LPS treatment results in a significant increase in ROS levels that is reflected in the higher average percent oxidation of cysteines (Figure 5F).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
